<% content_for :title, "Installation" %>

<div class="mb-8">
  <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Getting Started</p>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-3">Installation</h1>
  <p class="text-muted-foreground">Add rails_ui_kit to your Rails app in four steps.</p>
</div>

<div class="mb-10 rounded-lg border border-border bg-background px-5 py-4">
  <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
    <span class="font-semibold text-foreground">Requires Tailwind CSS 4</span>, through
    <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">tailwindcss-rails</code> 4.x.
    Every colour and radius in the kit comes from design tokens that only a Tailwind 4 build can compile — there is no
    Tailwind 3, PostCSS or Sprockets path for them.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="space-y-10">

  <div>
    <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-lg font-semibold mb-4">
      <span class="flex h-6 w-6 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-foreground text-background text-xs font-bold">1</span>
      Add to your Gemfile
    </h2>
    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
        <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">Gemfile</span>
        <button data-copy-target="#install-gem" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="install-gem" class="text-card-foreground">gem "rails_ui_kit", git: "git@github.com:productmatter/rails-ui-kit.git", tag: "v<%= RailsUiKit::VERSION %>"</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Then run <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">bundle install</code>.
      The kit isn't on RubyGems: it installs from the private GitHub repository, pinned to a release tag, so an upgrade is
      a change you make on purpose. On CI, use the HTTPS URL
      (<code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">https://github.com/productmatter/rails-ui-kit.git</code>)
      with <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">BUNDLE_GITHUB__COM</code> set to a token that can read the repo.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-lg font-semibold mb-4">
      <span class="flex h-6 w-6 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-foreground text-background text-xs font-bold">2</span>
      Run the install generator
    </h2>
    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
        <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">Terminal</span>
        <button data-copy-target="#install-gen" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="install-gen" class="text-card-foreground">bin/rails generate rails_ui_kit:install</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      This wires the JavaScript controllers into <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">application.js</code>,
      imports the built CSS into your Tailwind entry point, and adds
      <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">@custom-variant dark (&amp;:where(.dark, .dark *));</code>
      so Tailwind's <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">dark:</code> utilities follow the same
      <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">.dark</code> class the kit's tokens do, rather than the
      OS preference. If your stylesheet already defines a dark variant, the generator leaves it alone and says so.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-lg font-semibold mb-4">
      <span class="flex h-6 w-6 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-foreground text-background text-xs font-bold">3</span>
      Set up your layout
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
      Put <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">bg-background text-foreground</code> on
      <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">&lt;body&gt;</code>. The kit paints no page colour
      of its own, so without it your page keeps the browser's white under dark-mode components, and kit surfaces won't match the page around them.
      Then render the singletons once — they provide the global confirm dialog, toast stack, utility controllers, and the
      container a Turbo Stream renders a modal into (see <%= link_to "Modal & Turbo", modal_turbo_path, class: "underline" %>).
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
        <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">app/views/layouts/application.html.erb</span>
        <button data-copy-target="#install-layout" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="install-layout" class="text-card-foreground">&lt;body class="bg-background text-foreground"&gt;
  &lt;%= render Ui::ConfirmDialogComponent.new %&gt;
  &lt;%= render Ui::ToastContainerComponent.new(flash: flash) %&gt;
  &lt;div data-controller="ui--turbo-confirm ui--turbo-disable-with"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;%# Where Turbo Streams open modals. data-turbo-permanent keeps an open one through a morph. %&gt;
  &lt;div id="modal" data-turbo-permanent&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;%= yield %&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-lg font-semibold mb-4">
      <span class="flex h-6 w-6 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-foreground text-background text-xs font-bold">4</span>
      Install peer dependencies
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
      Dropdown, Popover, Tooltip and Select position themselves with
      <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">@floating-ui/dom</code>, and the kit's JavaScript entry
      point imports it whichever components you use. With importmap (the default) there is nothing to do: the engine pins it for you.
      With a JavaScript bundler (esbuild, webpack, rollup), install it alongside the kit's own package, which comes from the same
      GitHub repository, then call <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">registerControllers(application)</code>
      from <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">rails-ui-kit</code> after
      <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">Application.start()</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
        <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">Terminal</span>
        <button data-copy-target="#install-npm" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="install-npm" class="text-card-foreground">npm install @floating-ui/dom @hotwired/stimulus
npm install github:productmatter/rails-ui-kit#v<%= RailsUiKit::VERSION %></code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
